upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about factors of industrial location, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["A cheap and abundant labour force is always a locational advantage, regardless of its skill level or efficiency","No single factor decides the location of an industry; factors are complementary and often offset one another","Modern industries require a wide variety of raw materials, and not all can be sourced locally, making imports necessary","Coalfields remain major industrial areas today partly due to industrial inertia, even though coal is no longer as decisive a locational factor as before"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the text's actual point — it explicitly states a cheap, abundant labour force is NOT always a locational advantage unless the labour is also efficient, since low output or inefficiency can offset the cost saving. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about industrial fuel/power as a locational factor, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. In the early Industrial Revolution, roughly twelve tonnes of coal were needed to smelt one tonne of iron ore, making coalfield locations almost indisputable.\n2. With improved fuel efficiency requiring only about one tonne of coal per tonne of iron smelted, coal's pull on location weakened, yet many industries remained at their original coalfield sites.\n3. Petroleum-producing regions like the Middle East and Venezuela have historically become major manufacturing centres precisely because of their abundant oil supply.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual point — the text explicitly states petroleum is seldom a factor of industrial location, and regions like the Middle East and Venezuela have FEW manufacturing industries despite abundant oil. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, what specific term describes the phenomenon whereby industries remain at their traditional sites even after the original locational advantage (such as coal) has diminished in importance?","o":["Industrial inertia","Industrial decentralization","Industrial dispersal","Footloose industry"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly names this phenomenon 'industrial inertia' — explained by the built-up skilled labour force and the huge expense of relocating communications, workshops and worker housing to a new site, not decentralization, dispersal, or footloose industry (a term describing industries with no strong locational pull at all)."})

