upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Type of Iron Ore) with List-II (Characteristic, per Goh Cheng Leong) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Magnetite\nB. Haematite\nC. Limonite\nD. Siderite\nList-II:\n1. Low-grade residual ore with 20-30% metallic content, e.g. in Cleveland Hills and Lorraine\n2. Richest ore (70% metallic content), black, of igneous origin, e.g. Kiruna, Sweden\n3. Commonest commercially workable ore, brown, sometimes called 'bog iron ore'\n4. High-grade sedimentary ore (50-65% content), reddish, e.g. Lake Superior\nCodes:","o":["A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1","A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1: magnetite is the richest, igneous-origin ore (Kiruna); haematite is high-grade and sedimentary (Lake Superior); limonite is the commonest workable, brown 'bog iron ore'; and siderite is the lowest-grade residual ore (Cleveland Hills, Lorraine)."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about steelmaking processes, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["The original Bessemer acid process, invented in 1856, effectively removes phosphorus impurities from pig iron, making it suitable for phosphorus-rich ores","The basic Bessemer or Gilchrist-Thomas process (1878) was specifically developed to remove phosphorus and sulphur impurities that the original Bessemer process could not remove","The open-hearth (Martin-Siemens) process is slower (about 12 hours) but produces high-quality steel and can use scrap iron","The oxygen process uses a high-pressure jet of oxygen rather than air, produces excellent steel in about an hour, and accounts for about 75% of Japan's steel output"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual limitation — the text explicitly states the ORIGINAL Bessemer process does NOT remove phosphorus impurities, which is precisely why the basic Bessemer (Gilchrist-Thomas) process was later invented in 1878 to address this gap. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about iron/steel alloying, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Alloying iron with chromium retards rusting, as in stainless steel used for cutlery.\n2. Alloying iron with vanadium improves its magnetic qualities, useful for high-speed cutting tools.\n3. Alloying iron with manganese makes it tougher, suitable for manufacturing steam rollers.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 2 is incorrect and misattributes the effect — the text states COBALT (not vanadium) improves iron's magnetic qualities for high-speed cutting tools, while vanadium instead makes iron highly resilient, useful for springs. Statements 1 and 3 are correct."})

