upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the changing historical location of the world's iron and steel industry, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. In the earliest phase (15th century), iron-making yards were sited near forests and hillsides, using wood, wind (for hillside 'blast') and water.\n2. In the 18th century, the industry moved to coalfields when smelting required about 8 tonnes of coal per tonne of iron.\n3. By the late 19th century, with improved efficiency (1 tonne of coal per tonne of iron), the industry moved back permanently to forested regions rather than to ore-fields.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text explicitly states that by the late 19th century, with the coalfield pull weakened, the industry moved to ORE-FIELDS (e.g., Lorraine, Cleveland Hills, the Urals), not back to forested regions, which had been abandoned in the earlier coalfield-migration phase. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about world iron ore and steel production (1977 data), per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["India was, at the time, the world's largest producer of both iron ore and crude steel, surpassing the U.S.S.R. and the U.S.A.","The U.S.S.R. led the world in both iron ore production (27%) and steel production (22% in 1977)","India produced 5.5% of the world's iron ore, mainly haematite from Bihar and Orissa, feeding steelworks at Jamshedpur, Durgapur, Rourkela and Bhilai","Japan's own iron ore (from Kamaishi and Kutchan) supplied only about a tenth of her industrial needs, forcing heavy dependence on imports and scrap"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the text clearly identifies the U.S.S.R. and the U.S.A. as the world's two leading producers of both iron ore and steel, with India producing only 5.5% of world iron ore — India was never described as the world's largest producer of either. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): Japan is one of the world's most industrialized countries for iron and steel despite having very little domestic iron ore or coal.\nReason (R): Japan's coastal steel mill locations (e.g., Yokohama, Kobe, Yawata) facilitate the import of raw materials from countries such as the Philippines and Australia.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — Goh Cheng Leong explicitly attributes Japan's steel success, despite meagre domestic ore/coal, to coastal mill locations that facilitate importing ore from the Philippines, Australia and elsewhere, since her own mines at Kamaishi/Kutchan supply barely a tenth of her needs."})

