upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about India's Second-Five-Year-Plan-era public sector steel plants, per NCERT — entirely new content not found in Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Rourkela, Bhilai and Durgapur were all set up in the 1956-61 period under Hindustan Steel Limited, later absorbed into the Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL) in 1973.\n2. Rourkela was built in collaboration with Germany, Bhilai with the U.S.S.R., and Durgapur with the United Kingdom.\n3. The Bokaro Steel Plant forms a 'Bokaro-Rourkela combine', where wagons carry iron ore one way and coal the other.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct, matching NCERT's account of the three Second-Plan-era collaborations, their respective foreign partners, and the Bokaro-Rourkela wagon-exchange arrangement."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the cotton textile industry in India, per NCERT, is NOT correct?","o":["Because cotton is a weight-losing raw material like iron ore, Indian cotton mills have always been located strictly at the site of cotton cultivation, never elsewhere","India's first modern cotton mill was established in Mumbai in 1854, aided by proximity to cotton-growing regions, port facilities, capital availability and abundant labour","By 2000, the decentralised sector (powerlooms and handlooms) produced the overwhelming majority of India's cotton cloth, while the organised mill sector's share had fallen to about 6%","Tamil Nadu's rapid cotton textile growth, especially around Coimbatore, was driven substantially by the availability of abundant hydel power rather than local cotton supply alone"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual fact — NCERT explicitly states cotton is a 'PURE' (non-weight-losing) raw material, unlike iron ore, so mill location was instead driven by market, labour cost, or power availability rather than being tied strictly to cotton-growing areas. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): The sugar industry in India requires factories to be located very close to sugarcane-growing fields.\nReason (R): Sugarcane's sucrose content begins to degrade rapidly after harvesting, and better sugar recovery depends on crushing the cane within 24 hours.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — NCERT explicitly links sugar factories' proximity to cane fields to sugarcane being a weight-losing crop whose sucrose content deteriorates during haulage, making 24-hour crushing essential for good sugar recovery, a much tighter raw-material constraint than for cotton textiles."})

